### Introduction

`disks/database.disk` is not part of the default global. You must `@load` it. `Sqlite` composes with the built-in `Database` type. `':memory:'` opens a database that lives only for this run. This database leaves no file on disk. `Self.memory (;)` is a static function. It is shorthand for `':memory:'`.
###
@load 'disks/database.disk'

db := Sqlite.memory()
@connect db   # establishes the actual connection


### create_table and table_exists?

A table's schema is a named Struct. Each member is one column. The member's type sets the column's type. `Primary_Key` marks a member as the table's primary key. Here, `id` uses this marker. `Primary_Key` is a small, provided type, like `String` or `Bool` (`disks/database.disk`).

`table_exists?` and `delete_table!` take that same schema Struct. They also accept a bare table-name Symbol instead, if you do not have the Struct on hand. When given a Struct, each method derives the real table name from the Struct's own name. It makes the name lowercase and plural. So a Struct named `Survivor` maps to the `survivors` table.
###
Survivor <
	id: Primary_Key
	name: String
>

db.create_table(Survivor)

Raft <
	id: Primary_Key
>

@assert db.table_exists?(Survivor) == true, 'create_table should make the table exist'
@assert db.table_exists?(Raft) == false, 'a table that was never created should not exist'


### tables

This method lists every table in the database.
###
@assert db.tables() == [:survivors], 'tables should list every table in the database'


### delete_table!

This method drops a table completely.
###
db.delete_table!(Survivor)
@assert db.table_exists?(Survivor) == false, 'delete_table! should remove the table'


### The Table ORM

`Table` (`disks/table.disk`) is an ActiveRecord-style ORM built on top of what's covered above. CRUD works through one instance: take a schema Struct, call `db2.find_or_create_table(Schema)` on it, and that single call returns one ready `Table` instance, already wired to the database and the schema -- no separate composition step, no static `Self.database` declaration. Every method below (`all`, `find`, `create`, and more) runs directly on this instance.
###
@load 'disks/table.disk'

db2 := @connect Sqlite.memory()

Passenger <
	id: Primary_Key
	name: String
	status: String
>

passengers := db2.find_or_create_table(Passenger)


### create

`create` takes a Struct of the columns to insert, not a Dictionary. It returns the created record as a Struct -- the `id` field fills in automatically.
###
jack   := passengers.create(<name := 'Jack', status := 'rescued'>)
kate   := passengers.create(<name := 'Kate', status := 'rescued'>)
sawyer := passengers.create(<name := 'Sawyer', status := 'missing'>)

@assert jack.id == 1, 'create should assign an id automatically'
@assert jack.name == 'Jack', 'create should return the created record'


### all

`all` fetches every row as an Array of Structs, each matching the table's own schema.
###
@assert passengers.all().length() == 3, 'all should fetch every row'


### find and find_by

`find` looks up one row by id. `find_by` takes a Struct of conditions and returns the first matching row, or nil when nothing matches.
###
@assert passengers.find(jack.id).name == 'Jack', 'find should look up a row by id'
@assert passengers.find_by(<name := 'Kate'>).id == kate.id, 'find_by should return the first row matching the conditions'
@assert passengers.find_by(<name := 'nobody'>) == nil, 'find_by should return nil when nothing matches'


### where

`where` works like `find_by`, but returns every matching row as an Array instead of only the first.
###
rescued := passengers.where(<status := 'rescued'>)
@assert rescued.length() == 2, 'where should return every matching row'


### update and delete

`update` takes an id and a Struct of the columns to change. `delete` removes one row by id.
###
passengers.update(jack.id, <name := 'Jack Shephard'>)
@assert passengers.find(jack.id).name == 'Jack Shephard', 'update should change the row in place'

passengers.delete(sawyer.id)
@assert passengers.all().length() == 2, 'delete should remove the row'
